      "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"Nielzabub\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/nielzabub/48/1986_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Nielzabub:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<div><b>Koey wrote:</b><br><blockquote>&nbsp;Nielzabub wrote:<br><blockquote>I would say yes, since part of the text on the Rod and the Idol say they enter a player's play area. Now if the pin or effect specifies the amount of hero cards in play, then neither the rod nor the idol would count since they are both still environment cards even if they're in a hero's play area.&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n</blockquote>\n&nbsp;So by this assumption. Jinxes and Pins would also count towards that?\n</blockquote></div>\nJinxes and pins don't enter a hero's play area. You put them next to them simply so you know which pin is affecting whom. They are still villain cards being played by the villain. I'd put Twist the Ether into this category as well since it gets destroyed when hero ongoings are destroyed. My deal with the Rod and Idol is that they specifically say they enter the hero's play area. They are not cards that are played on the hero.\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Interesting since I never noticed the difference before.&nbsp; I would also like to add the Talisman also acts like a Jinx except it also provides the Hero a power when \"in front\".&nbsp;  I also was unable to find any other reference to play area outside of those cards.</p>",
